## Statement

Let $k$ be a field of characteristic not $2$, let $V$ be a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space, and let $B:V\times V\to k$ be symmetric bilinear, possibly degenerate. Set $Q(v)=B(v,v)$ and let $\Lambda V$ be the exterior algebra of the finite free $k$-module $V$ ([[def-exterior-algebra-of-a-finite-free-module]]). Define $\epsilon_v(\omega)=v\wedge\omega$ and, on decomposable wedges,

$$\iota_v(w_1\wedge\cdots\wedge w_r)=\sum_{j=1}^r(-1)^{j-1}B(v,w_j)\,w_1\wedge\cdots\wedge\widehat{w_j}\wedge\cdots\wedge w_r,$$

with $\iota_v(1)=0$. Then $\iota_v$ is well defined and $k$-linear, and the operators $c(v)=\epsilon_v+\iota_v$ satisfy $c(v)^2=Q(v)I_{\Lambda V}$ for every $v\in V$, where $I_{\Lambda V}$ is the identity map on $\Lambda V$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A field $k$ of characteristic not $2$, a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space $V$, a symmetric bilinear form $B$ on $V$, and its diagonal quadratic form $Q(v)=B(v,v)$.

[F1] $T_k(V)$ is the direct sum of tensor powers with concatenation multiplication ([[def-tensor-algebra-of-a-vector-space]]).

[F2] $\Lambda V=T_k(V)/J_{\wedge}$, where $J_{\wedge}$ is the two-sided ideal generated by $j(w)j(w)$ for $w\in V$; multiplication is induced by concatenation ([[def-exterior-algebra-of-a-finite-free-module]]).

[F3] Every multilinear map on a finite list of modules factors uniquely through the corresponding tensor product ([[cor-finite-iterated-tensor-products-represent-multilinear-maps]]).

[F4] A two-sided ideal is an additive subgroup stable under multiplication on both the left and the right ([[def-left-right-and-two-sided-ideal]]).

## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 For each $r\ge1$, the displayed sum is multilinear in $(w_1,\ldots,w_r)$, so [F3] defines a linear map on $V^{\otimes r}$. Together with $\iota_v(1)=0$, these maps define $\delta_v:T_k(V)\to T_k(V)$. On a concatenated pair of pure tensors of degrees $p,q$, the first $p$ deletion terms give $\delta_v(x)y$, while each remaining deletion has the extra sign $(-1)^p$ and gives $(-1)^px\delta_v(y)$. Thus $\delta_v(xy)=\delta_v(x)y+(-1)^p x\delta_v(y)$; bilinearity extends this to homogeneous tensors. [F1, F3, construct]

1.2 Left multiplication gives $\epsilon_v\epsilon_w+\epsilon_w\epsilon_v=0$: in $\Lambda V$, $(v+w)^2=v^2=w^2=0$, so expansion gives $vw+wv=0$. [F2, algebra]

2.1 Let $S$ be the $k$-linear span of the terms $xj(u)^2y$ for $x,y\in T_k(V)$ and $u\in V$. This span contains all the squares, and multiplying its terms on the left or right only changes $x$ or $y$, so it is a two-sided ideal by [F4]. Every two-sided ideal containing all the squares contains $S$, hence $S=J_{\wedge}$ by [F2]. For $u\in V$, $\delta_v(j(u)j(u))=B(v,u)j(u)-j(u)B(v,u)=0$. If $x$ is homogeneous of degree $p$, then for homogeneous $y$ the derivation rule gives $\delta_v(xj(u)^2y)=\delta_v(x)j(u)^2y+(-1)^px\delta_v(j(u)^2)y+(-1)^{p+2}xj(u)^2\delta_v(y)$. Each outer term lies in $J_{\wedge}$ by [F4], and the middle term is zero. By linearity, $\delta_v(J_{\wedge})\subseteq J_{\wedge}$, so $\delta_v$ descends to $\iota_v$ on $\Lambda V$. Since $J_{\wedge}$ is a two-sided ideal, left multiplication by $j(v)$ also descends to $\epsilon_v$. [F2, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 On a decomposable wedge, every term of $\iota_v\iota_w+\iota_w\iota_v$ deletes two distinct positions. For fixed $i<j$ and fixed choice of which contraction deletes each factor, deleting position $i$ before $j$ changes the second deletion's sign from $(-1)^{j-1}$ to $(-1)^{j-2}$; the two orders therefore have opposite signs and identical scalar coefficient. Pairing all such terms gives zero. If $r<2$, both compositions vanish. [step 2.1, algebra]

3.2 The contraction formula separates the term deleting the first factor and the terms deleting the remaining factors, giving $\iota_v(w\wedge\omega)=B(v,w)\omega-w\wedge\iota_v(\omega)$ on decomposable $\omega$ and hence on all $\omega$. Thus $\iota_v\epsilon_w+\epsilon_w\iota_v=B(v,w)I$. [step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 Adding the three anticommutator identities yields $c(v)c(w)+c(w)c(v)=(B(v,w)+B(w,v))I=2B(v,w)I$. Taking $w=v$ and cancelling $2$ in $k$ gives $c(v)^2=Q(v)I$. Linearity of $\epsilon_v$ and of the contraction formula in $v$ makes $c:V\to\operatorname{End}_k(\Lambda V)$ linear. [given, step 1.2, step 3.1, step 3.2, algebra] ∎
